From bd0d17098de75137fe1235b2b8ba58306f3f86c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 01:08:53 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 24/43] test(chexc): follow the narrowed rules -- one clause,
 casts rejected, subclass coverage

- parse/neg_duplicate_clause.cj returns (throws, throws across a 'where', captures);
  decl_multiple_clauses.cj and neg_empty_clause_combined.cj are gone with the rule
  they pinned.
- types/cast_target_neg.cj now covers every position a function type can occupy in a
  target -- itself, a result, a parameter, a tuple component, a type argument, a type
  pattern, and one written with a clause -- plus the parameter-typed target that stays
  accepted. cast_pessimistic{,_neg}.cj are gone: the reading they tested is now an
  extension.
- captures/captures_subclass.cj is new and captures_subclass_neg.cj is rewritten: a
  subclass restates coverage and adds its own, a more general entry covers a narrower
  superclass one, and both a missing clause and a non-covering one are errors.
- captures/captures_empty_and_ctor_neg.cj: 'captures ()' is not capturing (a primary
  constructor is fine there), and a capturing class without an 'init' is an error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
